    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The second disjunct drops the explicit clause ¬φ ∉ K. This does not change its truth conditions under Def3: if σ is true, its negation is false and hence cannot belong to sound K. But the source states the two-sided conclusion and argues for that clause specifically; Target states only one-sided nonmembership."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The passage concerns a designated K representing what the human mind can establish. Target generalizes to every sound set of arithmetic sentences. The intended K can be an instance, but membership has no epistemic interpretation built in, and for an arbitrary K a missing sentence may even be derivable from K."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Relative to Gödel's verbal conclusion, the witness need not be a Diophantine problem of the specified type: it can be any arithmetic sentence. Target gives neither a restriction on the witness nor a reduction producing such a problem. This restriction is also not explicit in Koellner's displayed φ-formulation."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "As a formalization of the argument, Target only defines a proposition; it does not prove it. In particular, it gives no derivation that starts with a hypothesized F* matching K, applies incompleteness to obtain a proper subset of arithmetic truth, and transfers the resulting witness back to K."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Target retains soundness and the inclusive disjunction, but replaces exact formal-system capture with enumerability of an arbitrary set. Its witness is implicitly two-sided by soundness, yet the definition neither establishes the passage's argument nor supplies its Diophantine conclusion."
